# attest_tag configuration, commented. guide/configuration.md is the full reference, and
# deploy/env/selfhost.env.example the short list a self-hosted deployment starts from.
#
# A self-hosted deployment keeps its settings in .env and, on Cloud Run, deploys with
# ENV_FILE=.env ./deploy/gcp/cloudrun.sh. .env.prod is the hosted service's own file, not a name to
# reuse: cloudrun.sh reads it in preference to .env, and the hosted service's public policy — open
# signup, a support address and website at attesttag.com, a $5 free-plan cap,
# ORG_MODEL_KEYS=enterprise — is meant for that deployment alone. .env.testing is a local
# development copy (start_dev.sh, a bare ./attesttag, the live tests). ENV_FILE names the file
# explicitly; without it the binary loads .env.testing when it exists, else .env.
#
# SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET, SLACK_CLIENT_ID, SLACK_CLIENT_SECRET and an LLM key are required; in the
# container image and on Cloud Run, MASTER_KEY too. Commented lines show defaults or optional
# settings. When you set one, leave nothing after the value: deploy/gcp/cloudrun.sh reads
# everything after the = as the value, a trailing comment included. Bot tokens are not set here:
# each Slack workspace is connected from the console (Workspaces → Add workspace → Slack, at
# /admin/workspaces) and its token is stored sealed, so one process can serve several workspaces.
#
# Values marked [console: key] are defaults only. The admin console's Settings page stores
# overrides in the database under that key, and those win over the environment without a restart.

# ── Slack (api.slack.com/apps → your app) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Basic Information → App Credentials → Signing Secret. Authenticates HTTP deliveries before
# processing any workspace data. Events URL: https://<your-host>/slack/events; Interactivity URL:
# https://<your-host>/slack/interactions. Disable Socket Mode: no app-level xapp token is needed.
SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=

# ── Microsoft Teams (optional; guide/msteams.md) ───────────────────────────────────────
# The same bot in Teams channels and chats, beside Slack. Unset all of these and Teams is not
# offered. Half-configured is a startup error, as it is for the GitHub App. Register an Azure
# Bot with its messaging endpoint at https://<your-host>/msteams/messages and the Microsoft
# Teams channel turned on; each tenant then installs the app package the console generates.
#MSTEAMS_APP_ID=                  # Azure Bot → Configuration → Microsoft App ID
#MSTEAMS_APP_PASSWORD=            # a client secret of that app registration (Certificates & secrets).
                                  # The only secret among these: the ids are in the app package.
#MSTEAMS_TENANT_ID=               # the directory the app registration is in; required for SingleTenant
#MSTEAMS_APP_TYPE=SingleTenant    # SingleTenant | MultiTenant — whatever the Azure Bot says it is
#MSTEAMS_SIGNIN=                  # Sign in with Microsoft: organizations (any work or school account) or one
                                  # tenant id. Unset, no button. Needs https://<your-host>/api/auth/microsoft/callback
                                  # as a Web redirect URI on the same app registration.

# ── Model provider: any OpenAI-compatible endpoint. Defaults target OpenRouter. ─────────
# The key (sk-or-… on OpenRouter). LLM_API_KEY is also accepted and wins when both are set. A value
# already exported in your shell beats this file, and the bot logs a warning with both fingerprints
# at startup.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
#LLM_API_KEY=
#LLM_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
#LLM_MODEL=z-ai/glm-5.3-flash               # [console: model] default chat model
#HEAVY_MODEL=z-ai/glm-5.3                   # [console: heavy_model] the Advanced model: fix jobs, and channels or threads that ask for it
#EMBED_MODEL=qwen/qwen3-embedding-8b       # [console: embed_model] embeddings for document search
#DENY_TRAINING=1                  # OpenRouter only: refuse providers that train on prompts (0 to allow)
#PROVIDER_SORT=price              # OpenRouter's provider routing: price | throughput | latency pins one of a model's
                                  # endpoints, which keeps the prompt cache warm across a turn's rounds; none leaves
                                  # OpenRouter's load balancing on. Fallbacks stay on either way.
#PROVIDER_ORDER=                  # providers to try first, comma separated
#ORG_MODEL_KEYS=all               # who may bring their own model key in Settings → Models: all | enterprise | off.
                                  # While one is in use every model call that organisation makes goes to it, and
                                  # nothing falls back to the key above (guide/plans.md#its-own-model-key)

# ── Storage and knowledge ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#DATABASE_URL=                    # postgres://user:pass@host:5432/attesttag?sslmode=require
                                  # Set this and the deployment uses Postgres; leave it empty and
                                  # it uses SQLite at DB_PATH. DATABASE_URL wins when both are
                                  # set, so a cutover — and its rollback — is one variable rather
                                  # than a different build. Postgres is what more than one
                                  # instance needs: SQLite allows exactly one writer and wants a
                                  # local block device, never NFS or SMB.
#REQUIRE_POSTGRES=                # 1 refuses to start unless the database is Postgres, so a DATABASE_URL that
                                  # fails to arrive stops the container instead of opening an empty SQLite file.
                                  # deploy/gcp/cloudrun.sh sets it whenever it sets DATABASE_URL.
#DB_PATH=attesttag.db             # SQLite file. In the container image: /data/attesttag.db, replicated by Litestream
                                  # when a bucket is named. In .env.testing set DB_PATH=testing.db so local runs
                                  # keep their own rows
#MAINTENANCE=                     # 1 is the cutover freeze (deploy/docs/postgres-cutover.md): Slack deliveries are
                                  # acknowledged and dropped, Stripe's are refused so they come again, no
                                  # background loop starts, and the console says so. Unset it to resume.
# Documents live in a folder by default. Name an object store instead and they live there —
# which is what a platform with no persistent disk (Cloudflare Containers) or no FUSE mount
# (Fargate, Container Apps) needs. S3-compatible covers AWS S3, Cloudflare R2, MinIO,
# DigitalOcean Spaces, Backblaze B2 and Wasabi; DOCS_S3_URL wins over DOCS_BUCKET if both are set.
#DOCS_S3_URL=                     # s3://bucket/prefix?endpoint=https://…&region=auto
                                  # endpoint may be omitted on AWS itself (derived from region);
                                  # region defaults to us-east-1 when left off — R2 wants "auto".
                                  # Each organisation's documents go under <prefix>/org-<id>/.
#DOCS_S3_KEY_ID=
#DOCS_S3_SECRET=
                                  # A bucket here also replicates the SQLite database into it
                                  # (see LITESTREAM_* below), unless DATABASE_URL is set.
#DOCS_DIR=docs                    # where documents live with no bucket named (/app/docs in the container image),
                                  # one folder per organisation, org-<id>/, indexed for search_docs:
                                  # .md .markdown .txt .rst .pdf .html .htm .csv .json. A file at the top of it
                                  # belongs to no organisation and is never indexed.
                                  # PDFs (folder and thread attachments) need `pdftotext` from poppler on PATH;
                                  # a PDF is refused past 32 MiB of text or a minute of pdftotext.

# ── Behaviour defaults (the [console: key] ones are overridable there; the rest are the operator's) ──
#BOT_NAME=attest_tag              # [console: bot_name] how the bot refers to itself in the system prompt
#TZ_NAME=America/New_York         # [console: timezone] cron timezone for routines and "current time" in prompts;
                                 # a new organisation takes the zone its founder signed up from instead
#MONTHLY_BUDGET_USD=20            # [console: monthly_budget_usd] an organisation's monthly cap; the bot pauses when spend
                                 # passes it. 0 = the plan's or the operator's ceiling, and no cap only where there is none
#HISTORY_LIMIT=40                 # [console: history_limit] recent thread messages sent verbatim; older ones are summarised
#MAX_TOOL_ROUNDS=50               # [console: max_tool_rounds] tool-calling rounds per turn before the bot answers with what it has.
                                 # A channel may raise its own on its Configure page; no reply gets more than 80
#TURN_MAX_MINUTES=12              # ceiling on one reply's wall clock. Investigations and routines run on clocks of their own
                                 # (investigation_minutes, routine_minutes), which this does not shorten
#TURN_MAX_USD=0.50                # most one turn — a reply, an investigation or a routine run — may spend before it stops
                                 # digging and writes up what it has (0 = no ceiling). Where a provider reports no cost,
                                 # eight million prompt tokens stands in.
                                 # The operator's, not an organisation's: rounds bound how long a turn digs, this bounds
                                 # what the digging costs, and the two come apart because every round re-sends the whole
                                 # transcript — so a turn that loops gets more expensive the longer it loops
#ROUTINE_ROUNDS=200               # [console: routine_rounds] tool rounds one scheduled run may spend, held to 1–200
#ROUTINE_MINUTES=30               # [console: routine_minutes] and the clock it spends them in, held to 2–60
#INVESTIGATION_WORKERS=2          # investigations this container runs at once, across every organisation. 0 switches the
                                 # lane off here; queued questions then wait for a container that runs one

# ── HTTP server: /slack/events, /slack/interactions, admin console, /api, /health ──────────────────────
#HEALTH_ADDR=:8080                # listen address; defaults to ":$PORT" (Cloud Run sets PORT)
#PORT=8080
#ADMIN_BASE_URL=                  # public URL of this server, no trailing slash, e.g. https://bot.example.com.
                                  # Pins the origin behind every link the bot builds — reply footers, setup links,
                                  # verification, reset and invitation mail, the worker callback — and every
                                  # sign-in and OAuth redirect. Unset, the bot learns it from the first sign-in or
                                  # signed-in console request on a host that is not loopback, keeps it as the
                                  # public_origin setting, and afterwards lets only that host change it. A Slack or
                                  # Microsoft sign-in started on any other address is sent to that origin first.
                                  # Until one is learned, links and sign-in redirects point at
                                  # http://localhost:<port>, which only works on the bot's own machine.
#PUBLIC_ORIGIN_HOSTS=             # with ADMIN_BASE_URL unset, the hosts (comma separated) the origin may be learned
                                  # from, instead of the first one that arrives
#TRUST_PROXY=                     # whether X-Forwarded-For names the caller, for the sign-in and two-factor lockouts,
                                  # the signup, reset, checkout and operator limits and the audit log's address.
                                  # Unset: believed from a private or loopback peer and on Cloud Run, ignored from a
                                  # public one. 1 always believes it (a proxy that reaches the bot from a public
                                  # address); 0 never does. The rightmost address is the one read.

# ── Transactional email (optional, strongly recommended) ───────────────────────────────
# Verification links, password resets and invitations all go by email. Leave these unset and the
# bot still starts: it logs each message instead of sending it (and, in the container image and on
# Cloud Run, warns at startup), and the console offers the link to copy by hand — so nobody is
# locked out, they just get their links from you. Any non-empty key counts as one, so a placeholder
# left in with MAIL_FROM set sends every message to be refused.
#RESEND_API_KEY=                  # re_… from resend.com → API Keys
#MAIL_FROM=attest_tag <no-reply@mail.example.com>
                                  # The domain must be verified at resend.com/domains, with the
                                  # SPF and DKIM records it gives you added to DNS. Until then
                                  # Resend accepts the call and delivers to nobody but you.

# ── Admin console login ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# There is no environment account. People sign up, get an organisation, and invite each other;
# authority comes from a membership, never from a variable set on the process.
# SLACK_CLIENT_ID and SLACK_CLIENT_SECRET are required: without them no Slack workspace can be
# connected, nor Sign in with Slack offered, and the process will not start. Basic Information →
# App Credentials on the Slack app; then register BOTH <ADMIN_BASE_URL>/api/auth/callback and
# <ADMIN_BASE_URL>/slack/oauth/callback under OAuth & Permissions → Redirect URLs, and turn on
# Manage Distribution → Activate Public Distribution. Slack requires https for both.
SLACK_CLIENT_ID=
SLACK_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── Who may use the bot ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#ALLOWED_EMAIL_DOMAINS=           # the default for who the bot answers, in Slack and Teams, by email domain (comma or
                                  # space separated, e.g. example.com), for organisations that keep no list of their own:
                                  # each one's Settings → Security list replaces it, and a new one starts with its
                                  # founder's domain (not a public mail provider's). Guests, Slack Connect members and
                                  # accounts from another workspace are already refused unless allow_external_users is on
                                  # for the org; this narrows things further to named domains among your own members. With
                                  # a list in force, an account whose email the platform will not show is refused.

# ── Access requests ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Who may approve is not an env var: approval tiers live in the console, under Approvers. A tier
# has a rank, members (Slack user ids, Teams member ids or emails), and what it may grant from —
# whole bundles, and connections picked on their own; being listed on a tier is the whole
# authorisation. A write through a connection an admin marked "allow access grants" always goes
# to an approver. No tiers configured means the feature is off. Needs the im:write bot scope (the
# card is a DM) and users:read.email to resolve member addresses.
#ACCESS_TTL=                      # how long a request waits for an answer (default 168h). Mostly useful as
                                  # something short, like 2m, to watch the expiry sweep run.
#ACCESS_ALLOW_SELF_APPROVE=       # 1 lets the person who asked press Approve on their own request. For testing
                                  # the flow with one person: with it on, an access request is not an approval.
                                  # Self-approvals are recorded as such and announced in the thread.

# ── Secrets at rest ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#MASTER_KEY=                      # base64 of 32 random bytes (openssl rand -base64 32; a hex key is refused). Seals
                                  # stored connection credentials with AES-256-GCM and signs /configure links.
                                  # If missing, a bare binary generates one and APPENDS it to the dotenv file it
                                  # read; the container image (REQUIRE_MASTER_KEY=1) and Cloud Run refuse to start
                                  # instead. Back it up: without it every stored credential is unreadable.
#REQUIRE_MASTER_KEY=              # 1 (set in the container image) refuses to start without MASTER_KEY rather than
                                  # generate one. It also ignores LLM_DUMP_DIR, keeps session cookies Secure on every
                                  # origin unless INSECURE_COOKIES=1, and warns when RESEND_API_KEY is unset. Cloud
                                  # Run behaves the same without it.

# ── Documents on Cloud Run ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#DOCS_BUCKET=attesttag-data-<project>   # a GCS bucket for documents; uploads, listing and indexing all go through its
                                        # API, under <DOCS_PREFIX>/org-<id>/
#DOCS_PREFIX=docs                        # object prefix inside the bucket

# ── Replicating the SQLite database (container only) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Set nothing here and DOCS_S3_URL above is enough: the database is streamed to that same
# bucket, at <docs prefix>/LITESTREAM_PATH, and restored from it when a container starts with
# an empty disk. With no bucket anywhere, the database is the local file and nothing else —
# fine on a box whose volume you back up, lost on a platform with no persistent disk.
# Replication is not clustering: SQLite still allows exactly one writer, and the lease object
# beside the replica (<path>.lock.json) is what keeps that true across a restart.
#LITESTREAM_S3_URL=               # s3://bucket/key?endpoint=https://…&region=auto — a bucket of its
                                  # own for the database, when it should not share the documents' one. A path
                                  # in the URL is the replica's key, and LITESTREAM_PATH is then ignored
#LITESTREAM_S3_KEY_ID=            # falls back to DOCS_S3_KEY_ID / DOCS_S3_SECRET when unset
#LITESTREAM_S3_SECRET=
#LITESTREAM_BUCKET=               # a GCS bucket instead, which is what deploy/gcp/cloudrun.sh sets
#LITESTREAM_PATH=litestream/attesttag.db
                                  # the single-writer lease lives beside it, at <LITESTREAM_PATH>.lock.json
#LITESTREAM_BIN=litestream        # only to point at a litestream outside PATH
#LITESTREAM_CONFIG=               # your own litestream.yml; unset means the one rendered at startup
#GIT_COMMIT=                      # the commit this build came from, recorded in the write lease the running instance
                                  # holds (SQLite with a bucket only). The container image takes it from its
                                  # GIT_COMMIT build argument; deploy/gcp/cloudrun.sh sets it because source
                                  # deploys drop .git

# ── Development and testing ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#SELF_TEST=0                      # 1 = answer the bot's own messages tagged [selftest] (curl-driven tests, live evals)
#EVAL_CHANNEL=                    # channel id the live evals post into; defaults to the pilot channel in evals_test.go
#REASONING=low                    # reasoning effort sent to OpenRouter: minimal|low|medium|high, always with
                                  # exclude so thinking never reaches the answer. none = the old off switch,
                                  # which some endpoints refuse ("Reasoning is mandatory for this endpoint")
#LOG_LEVEL=info                   # debug for verbose output
#LLM_DUMP_DIR=                    # when set, every outgoing model request body is written here as req-*.json
                                  # (ignored in the container image and on Cloud Run)
#GITHUB_LIVE_TOKEN=               # a real GitHub token for TestReposForTokenLive / TestSavedTokenLive; unset skips them

# ── GitHub App (optional; internal/app/github_app.go) ──────────────────────────────────
# Lets a person install a GitHub App on their repositories from the console instead of pasting
# a personal access token. Entirely optional: unset all of these and the flow is not offered,
# and repositories are connected with a token as before.
#
# WARNING: a HALF-configured app is a startup error, on purpose — the alternative is a console
# showing an Install button that leads to a 500. Set the id, the slug and a key together, or
# none of them. The OAuth client is the exception: without it, existing installations keep
# working and only the Install button is hidden, which is a far better trade than refusing to
# start and taking Slack down with it.
#
# Create at github.com/settings/apps. The id and slug are public — the slug is in the install
# URL people click — so only the key and the client secret are secrets.
#GITHUB_APP_ID=
#GITHUB_APP_SLUG=                 # the name in the install URL, e.g. github.com/apps/<slug>
#GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_B64=      # base64 of the whole .pem: `base64 -i key.pem | tr -d '\n'`.
                                  # Preferred. A PEM is 28 lines and this file is read one line
                                  # at a time; base64 also has no commas, which --set-env-vars
                                  # would otherwise split on.
#GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=          # or the raw PEM with its newlines escaped as \n
#GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID=            # OAuth client, needed only to offer the Install button
#GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── Fix worker: "fix this and raise a PR" in a separate container (jobs.go, internal/worker) ──
#WORKER_MODE=off                  # off | workers | local.
                                  # off:     the start_fix_job tool is not offered at all.
                                  # workers: each job runs in a container of its own, and the bot works out where
                                  #          from the environment the platform gives it — KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST,
                                  #          the ECS task-role endpoint, CONTAINER_APP_NAME, K_SERVICE, else a
                                  #          Docker socket. It says which at boot, and refuses to start jobs if
                                  #          nothing here says where it is, rather than guessing.
                                  # local:   the bot runs `attesttag worker` as a subprocess, for development
                                  #          (needs git, Python 3.12 + uv and the qwen CLI on PATH)
                                  #
                                  # Naming the platform pins it instead, which is the escape hatch when detection
                                  # would be wrong, and what every .env written before `workers` already says:
                                  #   cloudrun  one Cloud Run Job execution per job (deploy/gcp/worker.sh)
                                  #   ecs       one Fargate task per job            (deploy/aws/worker.sh)
                                  #   aca       one Container Apps job execution    (deploy/azure/worker.sh)
                                  #   k8s       one batch/v1 Job per job            (helm --set worker.enabled=true)
                                  #   docker    one container on the host's daemon  (deploy/local/worker.yml) — this
                                  #             one needs the Docker socket, which is root on the host; read that file
                                  # ADMIN_BASE_URL must be https in every mode but local: the worker calls back to it.
                                  # deploy/docs/platforms.md#the-fix-job-worker is the whole of it.
#WORKER_JOB_NAME=attesttag-worker # the execution the platform starts: a Cloud Run job, an ECS task definition
                                  # (family, or family:revision), a Container Apps job
#WORKER_IMAGE=                    # k8s and docker only, where there is no job object to name: the worker image.
                                  # Required in those two — the bot writes the whole pod spec there. WORKER_JOB_NAME
                                  # wins over it when set.
#WORKER_CPU=2                     # k8s and docker only: cores and memory per job. Elsewhere the job or task
#WORKER_MEMORY=4Gi                # definition its deploy script created carries them.
#WORKER_PROJECT=                  # cloudrun: GCP project of the job; defaults to the metadata server's project
#WORKER_REGION=us-central1        # cloudrun: the job's region. ecs: the cluster's, defaulting to AWS_REGION, then
                                  # AWS_DEFAULT_REGION
#WORKER_ECS_CLUSTER=attesttag     # ecs: the cluster to run tasks on. The bot signs its ECS calls with the task role,
                                  # or with AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY (and AWS_SESSION_TOKEN) when
                                  # those are set, which win over it
#WORKER_ECS_SUBNETS=              # ecs: required — Fargate has no default placement. subnet-a,subnet-b
#WORKER_ECS_SECURITY_GROUPS=      # ecs: egress-only group for the task. Without one the VPC default is used,
                                  # which often has no egress rule, and the worker then hangs rather than failing
#WORKER_ECS_ASSIGN_PUBLIC_IP=1    # ecs: 0 when the subnets route out through a NAT
#WORKER_AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION=       # aca: both required. The ARM path of the Container Apps job to start. The bot
#WORKER_AZURE_RESOURCE_GROUP=     # authenticates with its managed identity (AZURE_CLIENT_ID picks a user-assigned
                                  # one), else with AZURE_TENANT_ID, AZURE_CLIENT_ID and AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET
#WORKER_K8S_NAMESPACE=            # k8s: where Jobs are created; empty means the bot's own namespace
#WORKER_K8S_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=      # k8s: the account the worker pod runs as. Grant it nothing
#WORKER_K8S_PULL_SECRET=          # k8s: imagePullSecret for a private registry
#WORKER_K8S_ARCH=amd64            # k8s: the node architecture worker pods are pinned to; the published worker
                                  # images are amd64 only. `any` pins nothing, for an image built for more
#WORKER_DOCKER_HOST=unix:///var/run/docker.sock   # docker: the Engine API socket
#WORKER_DOCKER_NETWORK=           # docker: the network the worker joins, so it can reach the bot by service name
#WORKER_SA_EMAIL=                 # cloudrun: the job's service account; its identity token is checked on callbacks
#WORKER_REQUIRE_OIDC=             # 1 (default in cloudrun mode) requires that identity token. 0 elsewhere, which
                                  # have no Google identity token: there the single-use job token is the whole of it
#WORKER_JOB_NAMES=                # route a repository to a heavier worker by the ecosystem recorded for it, e.g.
                                  # java=attesttag-worker-jvm,dotnet=attesttag-worker-jvm (java-gradle and java-maven
                                  # also answer to java). A job or task definition, or on k8s and docker an image;
                                  # anything not named runs on WORKER_JOB_NAME
#WORKER_CACHE_BUCKET=             # the GCS bucket for the per-repository dependency cache, handed to each job as
                                  # signed URLs (default: LITESTREAM_BUCKET)
#WORKER_CACHE=                    # off to do without the cache
#OPENROUTER_PROVISIONING_KEY=     # an OpenRouter management key (openrouter.ai/settings/provisioning-keys): the bot
                                  # mints one key per job capped at worker_job_budget_usd, reads the exact spend back
                                  # and deletes it. Without it the worker gets the shared key below, uncapped — and
                                  # with SIGNUP_MODE=open a fix job is refused instead. A worker runs the
                                  # repository's own code, so set this in production.
#WORKER_LLM_BASE_URL=             # model endpoint handed to the worker with the shared key (default: LLM_BASE_URL)
#WORKER_LLM_API_KEY=              # the shared key a worker gets when no provisioning key exists (default: the bot's)
#WORKER_ENGINE_API_KEY=           # extra engine credential (an Anthropic key) for the later claude_code engine
                                  # Live knobs are in the console under Settings → Workers: worker_engine
                                  # (qwen_code | fake), worker_model, worker_job_budget_usd, worker_timeout_minutes,
                                  # worker_max_jobs, worker_pr_draft (recorded only: every pull request is opened
                                  # as a draft), worker_branch_prefix, worker_branch_suffix,
                                  # worker_event_retention_days, worker_allow_rules.

# Retention is a console setting, not an env var: data_retention_days, per organisation, under
# Settings. 0 — the default — keeps everything. Anything from 7 to 3650 sweeps turns, usage,
# tool calls, proxy audit, artifacts and cached file text older than that, once a day.

# ---- Per-organisation ceilings (limits.go) ----
# Sized for a service with open signup, where these are what stands between a stranger with a
# script and the disk, the mail domain and the model key everyone shares. On a twenty-person
# deployment behind an invitation they are simply in the way. A value that is not a positive
# number is refused and logged rather than silently applied.
#LIMIT_SIGNUPS_PER_IP_PER_HOUR=5
#LIMIT_INVITES_PER_ORG_PER_HOUR=20
#LIMIT_INVITES_PER_ADDRESS_A_DAY=3
#LIMIT_EMAIL_TURNS_PER_CHANNEL_PER_HOUR=12   # forwarded emails one channel may turn into a turn in an hour
#LIMIT_EMAIL_AUTO_WRITES_PER_TURN=3          # writes one forwarded mail may run unattended, where the channel lets
                                             # its allow rules apply to forwarded email
#LIMIT_ROUTINES_PER_ORG=50
#LIMIT_DOCUMENTS_PER_ORG=2000
#LIMIT_CONNECTIONS_PER_ORG=100
#LIMIT_BUNDLES_PER_ORG=50
#LIMIT_MEMORIES_PER_ORG=500
#LIMIT_DRIVE_SYNCS_PER_ORG=25

# ---- Production hardening (2026-09) ----
# The operator's ceilings, which no organisation's own settings can lift. Set the first one on a
# deployment that lets strangers sign up (25, say): with it unset, a tenant that puts
# monthly_budget_usd=0 has no limit on the shared key. Free accounts are held to it too; a plan
# granted with its own budget is held to that instead, and an enterprise account, a pro account
# paying by credit and an organisation on its own model key are not held to it at all.
#PLATFORM_MONTHLY_BUDGET_USD_PER_ORG=0   # most model spend one organisation may put on the shared key per month (0 = none)
#PLATFORM_MAX_INFLIGHT_PER_ORG=8         # turns one organisation may have running at once on an instance (0 = no cap)
# This deployment's public website, if it has one. The console's Get started page fetches its
# recorded walkthroughs from there, and this is the one cross-origin its CSP allows. Setting it
# here is enough: the console is told the same origin on /api/me, so there is nothing to set at
# build time. Empty (the default) means the console talks to its own origin and nothing else,
# which is what a self-host wants.
#SITE_URL=
# Who may create an account (auth_password.go). Defaults to first-run.
#   first-run  the first sign-up founds this deployment; everybody after it arrives by invitation
#   open       anyone who can reach the console may sign up and found their own organisation.
#              What holds the door then is the tenant boundary, not the door. Only set this if
#              you meant to run a service for strangers.
#   closed     never, not even to bootstrap — for a deployment provisioned some other way
#SIGNUP_MODE=first-run
# The free plan (plans.go) and the operator. These three are for a service handing strangers a key
# somebody else pays for; a deployment spending its own key wants none of them, and all default to
# off.
#FREE_PLAN_BUDGET_USD=0           # what a free account may spend a month (the hosted service sets 5). 0 = no
                                  # free-plan cap, which is the default: an account is then held by the ceiling
                                  # above like everybody else. The operator (OPERATOR_SECRET, deploy/plan.sh)
                                  # moves an account to pro, and so does a subscription where billing is on.
#SUPPORT_EMAIL=                   # where a capped account is told to write, and where the console's Help button
                                  # mails the questions typed into it. Empty is the default and means this
                                  # deployment publishes no address: budget refusals then say the budget is fixed
                                  # without naming anyone, and neither the Upgrade button nor Help — both of which
                                  # send mail — is offered. Never leave somebody else's address here.
#OPERATOR_SECRET=                 # at least 16 characters: the bearer secret behind /api/operator/ and the /operator
                                  # pages, which list accounts, move them between plans, grant credit and set
                                  # enterprise deals. Unset or shorter, those routes do not exist.
# Self-serve billing (billing.go). For a service selling a plan on a key it pays for; a
# deployment spending its own key wants none of it and gets none — with these unset the billing
# routes do not exist, the console has no Billing tab, and no account has a credit floor.
#
# Both keys or neither: a deployment that can charge a card and cannot hear the result would take
# money and never credit it, so half a configuration switches billing off with a warning.
#   Stripe dashboard → Developers → API keys
#STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
#   Stripe dashboard → Developers → Webhooks → add endpoint <your-origin>/api/billing/webhook,
#   and select: checkout.session.completed, checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded,
#   payment_intent.succeeded, charge.refunded, invoice.paid, invoice.payment_failed,
#   customer.subscription.created, customer.subscription.updated, customer.subscription.deleted
#STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
#   The plan sizes a Pro fee is set by, in the order they are offered:
#   size=<price id>:<amount in minor units>[:<credit included each month, minor units>]. STRIPE_BANDS,
#   the old name, is read when this is unset. Stripe dashboard → Product catalogue → Prices. Also
#   switch on "Customers can switch plans" in Settings → Billing → Customer portal and list these
#   Prices there, or the console's Change size button opens a portal with no way to change size.
#   The amount rides along so the console can draw its picker without calling Stripe; it is what
#   the page prints, and Checkout charges the Price, so keep the two in step. A size with no
#   entry here is not offered at all; one listed with no Price id (over_250=:0) is offered as
#   "Talk to us" and refused at checkout. The ladder's labels, user ceilings and fix jobs a
#   month live in config.go beside the keys, not here.
#STRIPE_SIZES=upto_10=price_aaa:4900:500,upto_25=price_bbb:9900:1000,upto_50=price_ccc:19900:2000,upto_100=price_ddd:34900:3500,upto_250=price_eee:59900:6000,over_250=:0
#BILLING_CURRENCY=usd            # a payment in any other currency is refused rather than converted
#BILLING_TOPUP_MIN_USD=25        # one top-up's bounds, enforced server-side. An amount from a
#BILLING_TOPUP_MAX_USD=10000     # browser is never trusted: open signup plus a public checkout
#                                # is a card-testing target.
#BILLING_LOW_BALANCE_USD=10      # warn the account, in its alert channel and by mail, at this much
#                                # credit left, once per 72 hours.
# /health prints the writer state, workspace, document and chunk counts and the month's spend only
# to a caller sending this in an X-Health-Secret header (or as ?secret=, which lands in access
# logs); everyone else gets "ok".
#HEALTH_SECRET=
# Rotating MASTER_KEY: set the old value here, the new one in MASTER_KEY, deploy, and remove this
# once nothing sealed under the old key remains (bot tokens, connection secrets, TOTP secrets). A
# value moves to the new key only when it is written again, and nothing reports how many are left;
# Configure links, connect links and operator sign-ins issued under the old key stop working.
#MASTER_KEY_PREVIOUS=
# Session and CSRF cookies are Secure except where the public origin is plain http on loopback; in
# the container image and on Cloud Run they are Secure everywhere. INSECURE_COOKIES=1 forces
# plain-http cookies for a LAN test, in the image too, and must never be set in production.
# INSECURE_COOKIES=1
# The log mailer (no RESEND_API_KEY) logs recipients and subjects only; MAIL_DEBUG=1
# logs the body — the reset and invitation links — for a developer following them locally.
# MAIL_DEBUG=1
# Fix-job worker image: the worker runs as root and drops to a sandbox user for the repository's
# own code (installs, tests, the engine). WORKER_SANDBOX_UID belongs to the worker image
# (Dockerfile.worker sets 10002); the bot never reads it. WORKER_ALLOW_SHARED_UID=1 lets a worker in
# any mode but local run repository code as itself, and should not be set.
#WORKER_SANDBOX_UID=10002
